<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple Computer//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Runs /usr/share/blackb0x/loaddaemons.sh once per boot. That script loads
  every plist in /Library/LaunchDaemons and runs every script in /etc/rc.d,
  because this build's launchd does neither -- see the script's own header for
  the evidence, read off the real decrypted 12H1006 root filesystem.

  IT LIVES IN /System/Library/LaunchDaemons AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE POINT.
  That is the one directory the device's launchd actually scans (the binary
  contains that string and does not contain /Library/LaunchDaemons at all), so
  this is the only place a unit can sit and be certain to run. Putting it in
  /Library would be circular: it would need itself to have already run.

  WHY A GENERAL LOADER RATHER THAN MOVING ONE PLIST. sshd not starting was the
  symptom, and the first fix was to ship openssh's plist into /System as well.
  That worked for sshd and for nothing else. Five of the seven
  LaunchDaemon-shipping packages in debcache/ -- cydia included -- install into
  /Library/LaunchDaemons, and every one of them was equally dead on a tether
  boot. Fixing the loader fixes the class; fixing the plist fixed one member
  of it and would have had to be repeated for every package added later.

  It also removes a divergence rather than adding one. The earlier attempt
  overrode openssh's own plist to drop its socket activation, on the theory
  that inetdCompatibility was vestigial here and that SockServiceName "ssh"
  could not be resolved. Both turned out to be false: this launchd's binary
  contains inetdCompatibility, SockServiceName, Sockets and "inetd-compatible
  service with no sockets", and stock /etc/services is 677,972 bytes and
  carries `ssh 22/tcp`. openssh's plist was never broken -- it was never
  loaded. With that override gone, so are the `Replaces: openssh` and the hold
  that existed only to protect it.

  EXACTLY ONE ATTEMPT PER BOOT, and the keys that guarantee it are worth
  spelling out because two of them are doing different jobs.

    RunAtLoad        start it when launchd loads the directory.
    KeepAlive false  do not respawn it when it exits, for any reason.
    LaunchOnlyOnce   refuse to start it a second time within this boot, even
                     if something explicitly asks -- a `launchctl start`, or
                     this unit being loaded again by our own loop or by the
                     untether's, both of which walk directories and load what
                     they find.

  KeepAlive alone would not cover the last case; LaunchOnlyOnce is the key
  that makes a second *request* a no-op rather than a second run. It is
  understood by this launchd -- the string is present in /sbin/launchd, along
  with RunAtLoad, KeepAlive, ThrottleInterval and AbandonProcessGroup --
  though no stock plist on the image uses it.

  WHAT THE SINGLE ATTEMPT IS PROTECTING AGAINST: a boot where AMFI is still
  enforcing. Everything this script touches is unsigned -- /bin/sh itself is
  from the `bash` package -- so on such a boot the job cannot even exec, and
  the correct behaviour is to fail once and stop rather than to keep trying
  against a kernel that will keep refusing.

  The case that looks like a loss is not one. If AMFI is enforcing when this
  runs and the untether makes it permissive later in the same boot, we have
  spent our one attempt for nothing -- but the untether's own loop does this
  same work immediately after it wins, so the daemons still get loaded. There
  is no boot on which giving up early costs anything.

  AbandonProcessGroup is set for the same reason `orphan_commander` exists in
  this ecosystem at all: the whole point of this job is to start things that
  outlive it, and launchd would otherwise take the process group down when the
  script exits.

  It can take a while -- /etc/rc.d/daemonload waits on syslogd -- and nothing
  waits on it, so that is fine. Both streams go to a real file on the system
  partition, because the whole reason this bug survived so long is that the
  one job that should have reported it sent stderr to /dev/null.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
      <string>xyz.regulad.blackb0x.loaddaemons</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
      <array>
        <string>/bin/sh</string>
        <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/loaddaemons.sh</string>
      </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
      <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
      <false/>
    <key>LaunchOnlyOnce</key>
      <true/>
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
      <true/>
    <key>UserName</key>
      <string>root</string>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/loaddaemons.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/loaddaemons.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
